":"whether-infinitive","contextKey":"noun-clauses-3","fingerprint":"5136dbc9c3013679b8080cb3c15e2c1456b3ce969cf2615ae6522444029bf06d","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-questions-reference"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["Before a **to-infinitive** expressing a yes/no choice, use whether.","**Whether to** introduces the decision between doing and not doing the task.","Choose **whether**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"That does not introduce this whether-or-not choice before an infinitive.","B":"What to would mean choosing an object, but the task already supplies the object.","C":"**Whether to** introduces the decision between doing and not doing the task.","D":"If does not normally introduce a to-infinitive in this pattern."}}},{"id":"69aef307-3886-57fb-aed4-df588b975f12","topicSlug":"noun-clauses","level":"B2","prompt":"“____ is important to measure the garden fence carefully.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"They"},{"id":"B","text":"It"},{"id":"C","text":"There"},{"id":"D","text":"—"}],"answerId":"B","hint":"The real content comes in the infinitive; English still needs a **grammatical subject**.","explanation":"Preparatory **it** fills the subject position, with the important action expressed later by the infinitive.","keywords":["preparatory-it"],"skill":"preparatory-it","contextKey":"noun-clauses-3","fingerprint":"d73576350b74102240f5f469ee974115c8fa88bd0ce770a55da961e5c58b095f","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-questions-reference"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["The real content comes in the infinitive; English still needs a **grammatical subject**.","Preparatory **it** fills the subject position, with the important action expressed later by the infinitive.","Choose **It**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"They does not agree with is and does not serve as preparatory it.","B":"Preparatory **it** fills the subject position, with the important action expressed later by the infinitive.","C":"Existential there does not introduce this adjective + infinitive evaluation.","D":"An ordinary English statement needs a subject here."}}},{"id":"eb20bd42-9a1c-5392-ab97-75195c64fc0c","topicSlug":"noun-clauses","level":"B2","prompt":"Complete the content clause: “We know that she ____ the garden fence every week.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"to measure"},{"id":"B","text":"been measured"},{"id":"C","text":"measures"},{"id":"D","text":"measure"}],"answerId":"C","hint":"A **that-clause** has its own subject and finite verb.","explanation":"The that-clause is finite, so **she measures** uses present agreement.","keywords":["finite-that-clause"],"skill":"finite-that-clause","contextKey":"noun-clauses-3","fingerprint":"e2dba9c806010337f940f292aeb7261965f9068a12ac93141e5ab19c2f3ac1a2","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-questions-reference"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["A **that-clause** has its own subject and finite verb.","The that-clause is finite, so **she measures** uses present agreement.","Choose **measures**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"A to-infinitive is not a finite verb after she.","B":"Been would need an auxiliary and does not fit this active present-simple clause.","C":"The that-clause is finite, so **she measures** uses present agreement.","D":"The third-person singular subject requires -s in this affirmative clause."}}},{"id":"a3639ab0-b0bd-5840-a853-b616a365702f","topicSlug":"noun-clauses","level":"B2","prompt":"Choose the polite indirect question about the travel leaflet.","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Could you tell me why does she translate the travel leaflet?"},{"id":"B","text":"Could you tell me why she translate the travel leaflet?"},{"id":"C","text":"Could you tell me why she to translate the travel leaflet?"},{"id":"D","text":"Could you tell me why she translates the travel leaflet?"}],"answerId":"D","hint":"The outer clause is the question; the inner clause keeps **statement order**.","explanation":"Inside **why she ...**, the subject comes before the verb; does is not retained.","keywords":["indirect-question-order"],"skill":"indirect-question-order","contextKey":"noun-clauses-4","fingerprint":"aee911929553941786aa97b6b2f238fb1dd00c50b6cbac66c9739ed2de9cdbe0","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-questions-reference"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["The outer clause is the question; the inner clause keeps **statement order**.","Inside **why she ...**, the subject comes before the verb; does is not retained.","Choose **Could you tell me why she translates the travel leaflet?**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"This retains direct-question inversion inside the embedded clause.","B":"The singular third-person subject needs the -s verb in this present-simple clause.","C":"A to-infinitive cannot serve as the finite predicate after she.","D":"Inside **why she ...**, the subject comes before the verb; does is not retained."}}},{"id":"7924bda2-638d-5a75-ae96-ca1aacd435b3","topicSlug":"noun-clauses","level":"B2","prompt":"“We have not decided ____ to translate the travel leaflet today.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"what"},{"id":"B","text":"whether"},{"id":"C","text":"if"},{"id":"D","text":"that"}],"answerId":"B","hint":"Before a **to-infinitive** expressing a yes/no choice, use wh